{
    "response": "**The Climate Pledge** is a sustainability commitment co-founded by Amazon in 2019 with the overarching goal of reaching net-zero carbon emissions across global operations by 2040[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶287cc6a64ac815058d91f76aa46e9e84]. The initiative is designed to unite companies worldwide to drive collective action, foster cross-sector collaboration, and promote industry-wide decarbonization efforts[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶287cc6a64ac815058d91f76aa46e9e84].\n\nTo become a signatory, companies must agree to three core commitments:\n* Regularly report their greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions.\n* Implement operational changes to decarbonize their business processes.\n* Neutralize any remaining emissions using credible, verified offsets[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶287cc6a64ac815058d91f76aa46e9e84].\n\nBy the end of 2024, the pledge had expanded significantly, encompassing 549 companies spanning 46 countries and 60 distinct industries[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶8fdf278a128b0b78cd58f4ed17696d8c]. As the network of signatories grew, the initiative's strategic focus shifted in 2024 toward collaborative efforts, specifically launching joint action projects and small working groups to tackle shared industry challenges[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶f1cf628f1d70b816663681d99bab054f]. \n\nThe Climate Pledge supports and accelerates specific decarbonization initiatives to advance its goals:\n* **Science Club Cohort:** A working group focused on addressing sustainability measurement challenges[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶f1cf628f1d70b816663681d99bab054f].\n* **Laneshift:** A public-private initiative launched with a $10 million investment to accelerate the transition to zero-tailpipe-emission electric trucks and charging infrastructure across India, Brazil, and Mexico[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶1e36da42a35c241336af9252a4b78aa3].\n* **Lower-Carbon Concrete Buyers Alliance:** Co-launched to utilize a book-and-claim system that allows companies to claim environmental benefits of lower-carbon concrete in advance of delivery, helping to scale sustainable procurement[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶8334cec0b3da9ad71f5e68ba3a525d3f].\n* **Project JOULE:** An initiative aimed at building a renewable-powered electric vehicle (EV) charging station network in Bengaluru, India, targeting 6.2 MW of renewable capacity and support for 5,500 EVs by 2030[^1:doc-5501c9cdfc7b318b264a674230dfa432§root¶8334cec0b3da9ad71f5e68ba3a525d3f].",
